{"name":"Infrared Burn","hex":"#dd3333","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"infrared","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The electromagnetic radiation of a wavelength longer than visible light, but shorter than microwave radiation, having a wavelength between 700 nm and 1 mm.","A specific wavelength in this range."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a wavelength in the infrared spectrum.","Relating to very low energies or very large distances or time spans."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/infrared"},{"term":"burn","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A physical injury caused by heat, cold, electricity, radiation or caustic chemicals.","A mark left behind by a burn; a scorch or scar caused by a burn."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To be consumed by fire or in flames.","To contain a fire or coals."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/burn"}],"entries":[{"title":"Infrared","blocks":[{"text":"Infrared radiation is electromagnetic radiation (EMR) with wavelengths longer than that of visible light but shorter than microwaves. The infrared spectral band begins with the waves that are just longer than those of red light, so IR is, per definition, invisible to the human eye. IR is generally understood to include wavelengths from around 780 nm (380 THz) to 1 mm (300 GHz). IR is commonly divided between longer-wavelength thermal IR, emitted from terrestrial sources, and shorter-wavelength IR, or near IR, part of the solar spectrum. Longer IR wavelengths (30–100 μm) are sometimes included as part of the terahertz radiation band. Almost all black-body radiation from objects near room temperature is in the IR band. As a form of EMR, IR carries energy and momentum, exerts radiation pressure, and has properties corresponding to both those of a wave and of a particle, the photon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Ir_girl.png/330px-Ir_girl.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infrared","wikidata":"Q11388"},{"title":"Burn","blocks":[{"text":"A burn is an injury to skin, or other tissues, caused by heat, electricity, chemicals, friction, or ionizing radiation. Most burns are thermal burns caused by contact with heat from hot fluids, solids, or fire. Burns occur mainly in the home or the workplace. In the home, risks are associated with domestic kitchens, including stoves, flames, and hot liquids. In the workplace, risks are associated with fire and chemical and electric burns. Alcoholism and smoking are other risk factors. Burns can also occur as a result of self-harm or violence between people (assault)."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/87/Hand2ndburn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burn","wikidata":"Q170518"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Human-Infrared.jpg/330px-Human-Infrared.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Human-Infrared.jpg/330px-Human-Infrared.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHuman-Infrared.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Human-Visible.jpg/250px-Human-Visible.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9b/Human-Visible.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Human-Visible.jpg/250px-Human-Visible.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHuman-Visible.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/67/Effect_of_emissivity_on_apparent_temperature.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/67/Effect_of_emissivity_on_apparent_temperature.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Materials with higher emissivity appear closer to their true temperature than materials that reflect more of their different-temperature surroundings. In this thermal image, the more reflective ceramic cylinder, reflecting the cooler surroundings, appears to be colder than its cubic container (made of more emissive silicon carbide), while in fact, they have the same temperature.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEffect_of_emissivity_on_apparent_temperature.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Active-Infrared-Night-Vision.jpg/500px-Active-Infrared-Night-Vision.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Active-Infrared-Night-Vision.jpg/500px-Active-Infrared-Night-Vision.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Active-infrared night vision: the camera illuminates the scene at infrared wavelengths invisible to the human eye. Despite a dark back-lit scene, active-infrared night vision delivers identifying details, as seen on the display monitor.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AActive-Infrared-Night-Vision.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/STS-3_infrared_on_reentry.jpg/500px-STS-3_infrared_on_reentry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/STS-3_infrared_on_reentry.jpg/1280px-STS-3_infrared_on_reentry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/STS-3_infrared_on_reentry.jpg/500px-STS-3_infrared_on_reentry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Thermography helped to determine the temperature profile of the Space Shuttle thermal protection system during re-entry.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASTS-3_infrared_on_reentry.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/77/Specim_aisaowl_outdoor.png/500px-Specim_aisaowl_outdoor.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/77/Specim_aisaowl_outdoor.png/500px-Specim_aisaowl_outdoor.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Hyperspectral thermal infrared emission measurement, an outdoor scan in winter conditions, ambient temperature −15 °C, image produced with a Specim LWIR hyperspectral imager. Relative radiance spectra from various targets in the image are shown with arrows. The infrared spectra of the different objects such as the watch clasp have clearly distinctive characteristics. The contrast level indicates the temperature of the object.[36]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpecim_aisaowl_outdoor.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Blue_infrared_light.jpg/500px-Blue_infrared_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Blue_infrared_light.jpg/1280px-Blue_infrared_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Blue_infrared_light.jpg/500px-Blue_infrared_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Infrared light from the LED of a remote control as recorded by a digital camera","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlue_infrared_light.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Infrared_portrait_comparison.jpg/500px-Infrared_portrait_comparison.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Infrared_portrait_comparison.jpg/1280px-Infrared_portrait_comparison.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Infrared_portrait_comparison.jpg/500px-Infrared_portrait_comparison.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Reflected light photograph in various infrared spectra to illustrate the appearance as the wavelength of light changes","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AInfrared_portrait_comparison.jpg"}],"sounds":{"infrared":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/55/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-infrared.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/5/55/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-infrared.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-infrared.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/5/55/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-infrared.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-infrared.wav.ogg"}],"burn":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/68/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-burn.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/6/68/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-burn.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-burn.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/6/68/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-burn.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%2